{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee, one of the surgery team doctors. You're here because of epigastric pain that’s been going on for about a week and came back last night?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s right. It’s in my upper left side, kind of under the ribs, and it goes around to my back. It felt a little better when I ate at first, but now it’s just dull and constant."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay, and you’ve had this kind of pain before, related to your gastric bypass and ulcers?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, a few times. Last time it was a marginal ulcer near the GJ anastomosis. I’ve been on Protonix and Carafate, but it came back."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Got it. We’re going to keep you NPO for now—nothing by mouth—so your stomach can rest. We’ll start IV fluids and give you medications through the IV to help with the ulcer."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, but I’m not vomiting or anything. I’ve been tolerating my tube feeds just fine—60 mL per hour, 760 cc over 12 hours."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s good to hear, and we’ll restart those soon. But since the pain is similar to your prior ulcers, we want to make sure we treat it aggressively from the start."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Makes sense. What kind of treatment will I get?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:48:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re starting IV pantoprazole twice a day to reduce stomach acid, and you’ll keep taking sucralfate. We’ll also give you a PCA pump for pain control so you can manage it yourself."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:49:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve used those before. But last time, narcotics made me nauseous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good point. We’ll watch that closely. If the nausea returns, we may adjust the pain meds. We already ordered labs to check your iron and nutrition levels."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember last time my iron was low. I take ferrous sulfate daily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:52:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your labs came back—your iron is 25 and ferritin is only 5, both low. We’ll adjust your supplement to a liquid form, twice a day, to help it absorb better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:53:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, thanks. And my other meds—should I keep taking them?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:54:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re reviewing them. Your citalopram, quetiapine, and lorazepam are continued. But we’re consulting the chronic pain team about your nortriptyline and Effexor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:55:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Effexor’s been making me feel a bit off. If we can stop it, I’d appreciate that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:56:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The pain team agrees. We’re stopping Effexor and increasing your nortriptyline to 100 mg at night. It may help with both nerve pain and sleep."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:57:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That sounds good. I trust my psychiatrist will get the note?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2171-02-06 16:58:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ll send a summary to Dr. ___ so they’re up to date."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2171-02-06 17:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We also did a KUB X-ray to check for free air or blockage. Good news—it’s normal. No signs of perforation or obstruction."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2171-02-06 17:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. So it’s probably just the ulcer acting up again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2171-02-06 17:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Most likely. We’re not doing another endoscopy this time since your symptoms are classic and you’re responding to treatment."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2171-02-06 18:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Doctor, I’ve been feeling nauseous again, and I dry-heaved a couple times. I think the PCA is making it worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2171-02-06 18:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thanks for letting us know. We’ll discontinue the narcotics and rely more on the nortriptyline and other non-narcotic methods. We can give you ondansetron if the nausea continues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2171-02-06 18:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’d prefer that. I don’t want to be sick on top of the pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2171-02-07 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your pain has improved, and you’re no longer nauseous. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2171-02-07 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Much better. Still a little sore, but manageable. Can I eat something?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2171-02-07 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’re going to start you on a bariatric stage 3 diet—soft, moist foods. You’ll begin with small portions and see how you tolerate it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2171-02-07 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. And can I restart my tube feeds too?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2171-02-07 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, once you’re tolerating the diet, we’ll restart your cycled feeds at 60 mL per hour for 12 hours daily, same as before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2171-02-07 12:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Doctor, I ate the soft foods—mashed potatoes, scrambled eggs—and I feel fine. No pain or nausea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2171-02-07 12:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s excellent. We’ll keep you on the stage 3 diet and restart your tube feeds this afternoon. You’re making good progress."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2171-02-07 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just checking in. The team notes you’re ambulating well, eating without issues, and your pain is well-controlled without narcotics."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2171-02-07 15:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I feel ready to go home. What do I need to do after discharge?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2171-02-07 15:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll continue the stage 3 diet and advance to stage 4 and 5 as tolerated. Keep the cycled tube feeds going. Take pantoprazole twice a day, sucralfate four times a day, and the new iron liquid twice daily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2171-02-07 15:04:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my nortriptyline is now 100 mg at night?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2171-02-07 15:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Correct. And crush all pills before taking them—your stomach anatomy is different after bypass, so whole pills can get stuck."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2171-02-07 15:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. No NSAIDs, no straws, no gum—I remember from last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2171-02-07 15:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. And call your surgeon or come back if you develop fever, severe pain, vomiting, or can’t keep food down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2171-02-08 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done really well. We’re clearing you for discharge today. You’re afebrile, pain-controlled, eating well, and ambulating."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2171-02-08 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. When do I follow up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2171-02-08 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll follow up with Dr. ___ in the office to monitor your progress. We’ll send all the notes over."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2171-02-08 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Dr. Lee. I feel much better and ready to go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2171-02-08 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and don’t hesitate to reach out if anything changes."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2171-02-06 16:38:00",
    "admission_end": "2171-02-08 16:33:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted for recurrent epigastric abdominal pain radiating to the mid-back, consistent with prior gastrojejunal ulcers. She had a history of Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and previous marginal ulcers. Upon admission, she was kept NPO and started on IV pantoprazole and sucralfate. Pain was initially managed with a PCA, but narcotics were discontinued due to nausea and dry heaving. The chronic pain service recommended increasing nortriptyline from 50 mg to 100 mg nightly and stopping Effexor. Iron studies showed deficiency, prompting a switch to liquid ferrous sulfate twice daily. The patient’s pain improved, and she successfully advanced to a bariatric stage 3 diet without nausea or discomfort. Tube feeds were continued at 60 mL/hour over 12 hours daily. A KUB showed no free air or obstruction. She remained stable, afebrile, and ambulatory, and was discharged home with medication adjustments and follow-up arranged with her surgeon.",
    "problems": [
      "Abdominal pain, epigastric",
      "Gastrojejunal ulcer, unspecified as acute or chronic, without mention of hemorrhage or perforation, without mention of obstruction",
      "Bariatric surgery status",
      "Gastrostomy status",
      "Polycystic ovaries",
      "Morbid obesity",
      "Nausea alone",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified",
      "Anxiety state, unspecified",
      "Long-term (current) use of other medications"
    ]
  }
}
